Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  
• form_log.session : key that ties together all page views made in the same visit   → GROUP / DISTINCT  
• form_log.path  : look for the two target pages  
           = '/regist/input' (1st page)  
           = '/regist/confirm' (2nd page)  
• form_log.stamp  : chronological order inside a session (text ISO‑8601 so lexical order = time order)  
Conditions that will be useful  
1. path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')  
2. Having both paths inside the same session  
3. MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'  THEN stamp END)  
   < MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END)  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually build up to the answer.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at every row whose path starts with /regist/
SELECT *
FROM form_log
WHERE path LIKE '/regist/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. What distinct pages are there under /regist/ ?
SELECT DISTINCT path
FROM form_log
WHERE path LIKE '/regist/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. See the first 20 sessions that ever hit /regist/input
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM form_log
WHERE path = '/regist/input'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. See the first 20 sessions that ever hit /regist/confirm
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM form_log
WHERE path = '/regist/confirm'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Sessions that contain BOTH pages (order not checked yet)
SELECT DISTINCT fl1.session
FROM form_log AS fl1
JOIN form_log AS fl2
      ON fl1.session = fl2.session
WHERE fl1.path = '/regist/input'
  AND fl2.path = '/regist/confirm'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Add the time condition: input time < confirm time
SELECT DISTINCT s.session
FROM
(
  SELECT
      session,
      MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'  THEN stamp END) AS first_input,
      MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
  GROUP BY session
) AS s
WHERE first_input IS NOT NULL
  AND first_confirm IS NOT NULL
  AND first_input < first_confirm          -- order check
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same logic as #6 but returning a straight count
SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_sessions
FROM
(
  SELECT
      session
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
  GROUP BY session
  HAVING MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'  THEN stamp END) 
       <  MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END)
) AS t;
```

```sql
-- 8. Self‑join method to enforce ordering (inner query limited)
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT a.session) AS n_sessions
FROM form_log AS a
JOIN form_log AS b
  ON a.session = b.session
WHERE a.path = '/regist/input'
  AND b.path = '/regist/confirm'
  AND a.stamp < b.stamp;
```

```sql
-- 9. Window‑function alternative (SQLite 3.25+)
WITH ordered AS (
  SELECT
    session,
    path,
    stamp,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY session ORDER BY stamp) AS rn
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
)
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT session) AS n_sessions
FROM ordered
GROUP BY session
HAVING MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/input'  THEN rn END)
     < MIN(CASE WHEN path='/regist/confirm' THEN rn END)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10. Group‑concat trick: build the ordered click stream per session
WITH seq AS (
  SELECT
    session,
    GROUP_CONCAT(path, '->' ORDER BY stamp) AS click_stream
  FROM form_log
  WHERE path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
  GROUP BY session
)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_sessions
FROM seq
WHERE click_stream LIKE '%/regist/input%/regist/confirm%';
```

Any of queries #7, #8, #9 or #10 will give you the final number of unique sessions that first visited “/regist/input” and later reached “/regist/confirm”.
